    dct:description "Undecidability reductions for equations with periodic functions are finite mathematical objects and can be checked now. Careful domain restrictions are essential, but no new empirical observations are needed." ;
    rdfs:comment "Proposed test with existing data: Check a reduction mapping an arbitrary infinite cellular-automaton evolution or halting instance to existence of a solution of an equation using integers, arithmetic, and trigonometric functions. Correct equivalence proves undecidability; a flaw in the encoding refutes the construction." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-gpt-5.6-sol-medium> ;
    delphi:evidenceAgainst "The statement depends on the exact equation language, domain, and meaning of 'finding a solution'.", "The contrast with pure polynomial equations changes when integer rather than real solutions are allowed." .
